.TH powerlinux-expander 1  "PowerLinux 0.1.0" "PowerLinux Manual"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
powerlinux\-expander \- Set up and toggle typed triggers (type ;date anywhere to expand it)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpowerlinux expander\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Set up and toggle typed triggers (type ;date anywhere to expand it)
.PP
Typed triggers read keyboard input through evdev and paste through a uinput virtual keyboard, which needs a one\-time setup with root rights. Only the last few typed characters are kept in memory; nothing is logged or stored.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBpowerlinux expander status\fR
Show whether typed triggers are enabled and can run
.TP
\fBpowerlinux expander setup [\-\-apply]\fR
Grant keyboard access: add you to the input group and allow /dev/uinput
.RS
\fB\-\-apply\fR: Run the commands with sudo instead of only printing them
.RE
.TP
\fBpowerlinux expander enable\fR
Turn typed triggers on (restarts the daemon if it is running)
.TP
\fBpowerlinux expander disable\fR
Turn typed triggers off (restarts the daemon if it is running)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH "HOW IT WORKS"
.PP
The daemon reads key presses from /dev/input and keeps the last 64 typed characters in memory. When they end with a snippet\*(Aqs trigger, it erases the trigger with Backspace through a uinput virtual keyboard and pastes the expansion, restoring the clipboard afterwards. Snippets with {arg:NAME} parameters open the picker\*(Aqs form instead.
.PP
Keys are decoded with a US layout, so triggers made of letters, digits and ; work on most layouts while punctuation may differ. A trigger that is a prefix of another (;d and ;date) fires first, so the longer one is unreachable.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
Setup adds you to the input group, which lets every program you run read keyboard input, not only PowerLinux. Typed text is never logged, written to disk or sent anywhere. Undo the setup by removing you from the group (gpasswd \-d $USER input) and deleting /etc/udev/rules.d/99\-powerlinux\-uinput.rules and /etc/modules\-load.d/powerlinux\-uinput.conf.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Review the setup commands, then run them:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBpowerlinux expander setup
powerlinux expander setup \-\-apply\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
After logging out and back in:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBpowerlinux expander enable\fR
.fi
.RE
